  "title": "Two Fractions: Area Word Problems",
  "intro": "Use overlapping partitions to teach area with two fractional side lengths.",
  "html": "<h2>Teaching notes for parents and teachers</h2><h3>How to teach it</h3><p>Divide a unit square in both directions and shade the overlap. Extend the model when a side is longer than one unit.</p><h3>Example to work through together</h3><p>Use the model below as you explain the example. Have your student point to the quantities as you discuss them.</p><div class=\"model two-fraction-area-model\"><p class=\"sample-equation\">2/3 × 3/4 = 6/12 = 1/2 square foot</p><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 340\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"One square foot split into three rows and four columns. A rectangle covering two rows and three columns has six of the twelve equal-area cells.\" style=\"max-width:400px;width:100%;height:auto\"><text x=\"190\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"currentColor\">Reference square: 1 ft × 1 ft</text><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"70\" y=\"44\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"130\" y=\"44\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"190\" y=\"44\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"250\" y=\"44\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"70\" y=\"124\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#217668\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"130\" y=\"124\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#217668\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"190\" y=\"124\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#217668\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"250\" y=\"124\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"70\" y=\"204\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#217668\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"130\" y=\"204\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#217668\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"190\" y=\"204\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#217668\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><rect class=\"area-cell\" x=\"250\" y=\"204\" width=\"60\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><path d=\"M58 124V284M53 124H63M53 284H63M70 297H250M70 292V302M250 292V302\" stroke=\"#154d56\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"8\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">2/3 ft</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"323\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">3/4 ft</text></svg><p>Each small cell is 1/3 foot tall and 1/4 foot wide, so twelve equal-area cells cover one square foot. The shaded rectangle contains six cells. The unshaded cells are part of the reference square, not part of the field.</p></div><h3>Ask your student</h3><blockquote><p>What fraction of a square foot is one small region?</p></blockquote><h3>If your student needs help</h3><p>If the student counts only one set of partitions, trace both directions on the grid.</p>",
